#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Translate a hyprlock.conf (hyprlang syntax) to swaywardlock KDL.

    hyprlock-to-kdl [CONFIG]      read a hyprlock config (default: hyprlock's search path)

Prints KDL on stdout. On stderr it prints the swaywardlock command line to use (`--immediate-render`
comes from general:immediate_render) and a warning for every key it could not carry over. It never
drops a key silently: a key with no KDL counterpart yet also becomes a `// hyprlock: key = value`
comment inside the node it belongs to, so the KDL itself shows what was left behind.

It understands hyprlang's syntax: sections, `$var = value`, `{{ a + b }}` expressions, `source =`,
`# hyprlang if/endif`, `\\` continuation lines, and comments with `##` for a literal `#`.

hyprlock defaults that differ from swaywardlock's are written out, so a translated config looks the
same: hide-cursor, fail-timeout-ms, the background colour, label alignment, the image border, the
fade durations, the blur offset and noise, vibrancy and vibrancy-darkness, and blur saturation 1
(hyprlock has none; ours defaults to swayward's 1.5). Two are left as ours: clear-input-after-ms (hyprlock
never clears a typed password; ours clears it after 10 s) and the input-field-* animations, which
are written only when an `animation` line sets them.

hyprlock text never reaches the output raw: control characters (CR, VT, FF, NEL, ...), U+2028,
U+2029 and bidi controls become \\u{hex} escapes in comments, strings and warnings, so a value
cannot end a `//` comment and inject KDL. Gradient angles are hyprlock degrees + 90, the closest CSS equivalent (swaywardlock
uses CSS angles; hyprlock's shader angle is not geometric, so only multiples of 90 are exact).
Relative widget paths become absolute against the conf file's directory: a deliberate divergence
(hyprlock uses the working directory when it locks; swaywardlock may chdir to /).
"""

